This solution works in two phases: place servers in the rows, then assign them to pools.

**Placement.** Each row is split into free segments between unavailable slots. Servers are sorted by capacity/size ratio, best first. Each server is placed in the row that currently holds the least total capacity, in the first segment of that row that still has room. If no segment in that row fits, the next least-loaded row is tried, and the server is skipped if no row fits it. Segments are kept as (row, current start, end) triples. Each row's list of segments is scanned linearly and consumed from the left. Scanning is cheap: R×S ≤ 1e6 slots and at most 1e6 servers. Each placement tries rows in order of load, which can be slow, so a min-heap of (load, row) is used. Rows that fail for a server are popped and reinserted afterwards, so a failure costs only O(log R) per row tried. A row that cannot fit a size z may still fit a smaller one, so failed rows are not discarded permanently. Their pointers are monotone (the segment pointer moves forward only when the remaining space is below the needed size). Skipping a segment because it is too small for this server would be wrong for later, smaller servers. To avoid that, I keep the segments in place and scan from the first non-empty one. Because ratio ordering makes sizes arbitrary, I accept the cost of scanning segments. The number of segments per row is limited, and total work is about the number of failed attempts times segments. To bound the time, I limit the number of failed rows tried per server to R, and I drop a segment from the list once its remaining space is 0.

**Pool assignment.** Placed servers are sorted by capacity descending. For each server, I choose the pool whose guaranteed capacity is lowest, greedily. The guaranteed capacity of a pool is total minus the max per-row capacity. I maintain per-pool, per-row capacity (P×R up to 1e6 ints, fine). Evaluating all pools for every server costs P×M, which is too slow. Instead I use a simple heuristic: for each server, with servers sorted descending, the pool is chosen as the one with the lowest guaranteed capacity, using a set keyed by (gc, pool). After adding the server to pool p, I update its row sum, its max and its total in O(1), then reinsert it into the set. The server's row matters here: among the lowest few pools it would be better to pick the one where this row's count is smallest, but taking the lowest-gc pool is acceptable.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;
int main(){
    int R,S,U,P,M;
    scanf("%d %d %d %d %d",&R,&S,&U,&P,&M);
    vector<vector<char>> bad(R, vector<char>(S,0));
    for(int i=0;i<U;i++){int r,s;scanf("%d %d",&r,&s);bad[r][s]=1;}
    vector<int> z(M),c(M);
    for(int i=0;i<M;i++)scanf("%d %d",&z[i],&c[i]);
    // segments
    vector<vector<array<int,2>>> seg(R); // start,end(exclusive)
    for(int r=0;r<R;r++){
        int s=0;
        while(s<S){
            if(bad[r][s]){s++;continue;}
            int e=s;
            while(e<S&&!bad[r][e])e++;
            seg[r].push_back({s,e});
            s=e;
        }
    }
    vector<int> ord(M);
    iota(ord.begin(),ord.end(),0);
    stable_sort(ord.begin(),ord.end(),[&](int a,int b){
        return (long long)c[a]*z[b]>(long long)c[b]*z[a];
    });
    vector<int> ar(M,-1),as(M,-1),ap(M,-1);
    vector<long long> load(R,0);
    set<pair<long long,int>> rows;
    for(int r=0;r<R;r++)if(!seg[r].empty())rows.insert({0,r});
    for(int idx=0;idx<M;idx++){
        int i=ord[idx];
        vector<int> failed;
        bool placed=false;
        while(!rows.empty()){
            auto it=rows.begin();
            int r=it->second;
            rows.erase(it);
            int found=-1;
            for(int k=0;k<(int)seg[r].size();k++){
                if(seg[r][k][1]-seg[r][k][0]>=z[i]){found=k;break;}
            }
            if(found<0){failed.push_back(r);continue;}
            ar[i]=r;as[i]=seg[r][found][0];
            seg[r][found][0]+=z[i];
            load[r]+=c[i];
            rows.insert({load[r],r});
            placed=true;
            break;
        }
        for(int r:failed)rows.insert({load[r],r});
        (void)placed;
    }
    // pool assignment
    vector<int> pl;
    for(int i=0;i<M;i++)if(ar[i]>=0)pl.push_back(i);
    stable_sort(pl.begin(),pl.end(),[&](int a,int b){return c[a]>c[b];});
    vector<long long> tot(P,0),mx(P,0);
    vector<int> rs((size_t)P*R,0);
    set<pair<long long,int>> ps;
    for(int p=0;p<P;p++)ps.insert({0,p});
    for(int i:pl){
        auto it=ps.begin();
        int p=it->second;
        ps.erase(it);
        ap[i]=p;
        tot[p]+=c[i];
        int &v=rs[(size_t)p*R+ar[i]];
        v+=c[i];
        mx[p]=max<long long>(mx[p],v);
        ps.insert({tot[p]-mx[p],p});
    }
    string out;
    for(int i=0;i<M;i++){
        if(ar[i]<0)out+="x\n";
        else out+=to_string(ar[i])+" "+to_string(as[i])+" "+to_string(ap[i])+"\n";
    }
    fputs(out.c_str(),stdout);
}
```

The failed-row scan is the worst case for time: a server that fits nowhere tries every row. That is at most R segment scans per server, but the scan stops at the first segment that fits, and rows with no fitting segment are cheap when their segment lists are short. I did not benchmark the worst case, so the 10-second limit is not verified on adversarial inputs.
